WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
Speaker 1: With Republicans taking over the House&nbsp;
of Representatives, we expect that the current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:09.360
House minority leader, Kevin McCarthy, is going&nbsp;
to become the next speaker of the House. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:15.540
he got the nomination of his party earlier this&nbsp;
month by a wide margin, 188 to 31, getting many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:20.700
more times the votes that his challenger,&nbsp;
Andy Biggs, got. But even so, McCarthy is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:26.460
going to have a tough time getting to that 218&nbsp;
vote threshold that you need to become speaker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:31.320
when that vote takes place on the House floor at&nbsp;
the start of next year. And that's because he has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:36.300
a slim majority to work with. Democrats are, of&nbsp;
course, going. To back Pelosi's successor. Hakeem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.560
Jeffries But he's not going to have the 218 votes&nbsp;
that he needs. Republicans are going to have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:46.380
decide among themselves who the next speaker of&nbsp;
the House is. So McCarthy will need nearly all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:52.020
the votes from the Republican caucus to get him&nbsp;
to that 218 threshold. He can only spare to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:57.000
about four Republicans vote against him. That's&nbsp;
the current projection based on how some of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:03.660
elections are playing out and how big the majority&nbsp;
is going to be for the Republicans. And that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:08.760
going to be a tall task for McCarthy, getting&nbsp;
those 31 Republicans who voted against him in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:14.580
nomination process to vote for him to speaker on&nbsp;
the House floor. He can only afford to lose a few.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:20.580
And right now, he's got to convince at least a few&nbsp;
dozen of them. We already have a number of hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:25.500
no's from Republican reps saying that they're not&nbsp;
going to be backing McCarthy. Many of these hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:32.040
no's come to us from the House Freedom Caucus.&nbsp;
They include people like Matt Gaetz, Ralph Norman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:38.520
Andy Biggs, Matt Rosendale and Representative&nbsp;
Bob Good. Interestingly enough, Marjorie Taylor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:43.500
GREENE not on this list. You'd think that she's&nbsp;
so fringe that of course she would back whoever is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:48.000
running against McCarthy. But maybe because she's&nbsp;
being promised her committee assignments back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.460
she is saying McCarthy is the person for the&nbsp;
job right now. And even among these hard no's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:57.660
are they actually hard nosed? Like they're&nbsp;
probably just using this for leverage in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:03.360
negotiation. The idea is that maybe they'll end&nbsp;
up giving their votes to McCarthy in exchange for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:07.860
some concessions. Like one thing that's being&nbsp;
floated in particular is to institute a motion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:13.140
to vacate the chair, which would allow members&nbsp;
of the House to kick out the current Speaker of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:18.240
the House mid-session. So if they got this in,&nbsp;
potentially what would happen would be McCarthy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.680
gets elected speaker, he gets enough votes, and&nbsp;
then after a short period, a period of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:28.860
the Freedom Caucus gets upset with him and decides&nbsp;
to oust him as their leader. He's apparently not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:33.300
in negotiations yet with the Freedom Caucus, but&nbsp;
his hand is eventually going to be forced, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:37.380
he's going to have to come to the table and talk&nbsp;
to these people if he wants their votes. McCarthy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:43.440
we know, has one of the speakership. For so long&nbsp;
it's been his ultimate goal and his career. And he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:50.280
nearly got it in 2015 when John Boehner decided to&nbsp;
step down as the leader of the House, Republicans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:55.380
stepped down as speaker of the House. But then it&nbsp;
became clear that McCarthy was not going to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.380 --> 00:03:01.260
the votes that he needed to become speaker. So in&nbsp;
came Paul Ryan, who was ushered in as someone who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:04.980
could represent, like the Tea Party factions&nbsp;
and the Freedom Caucus factions, while still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:12.660
having appeal to like the broader Republican Party&nbsp;
because he was Mitt Romney's VP candidate in 2012.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:17.880
And so he was the one guy everyone could agree&nbsp;
on. There's talk about that type of situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:22.920
happening against McCarthy and instead someone&nbsp;
like maybe Steve Scalise or Elise Stefanik or Jim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:28.440
Jordan coming in as the person that Republicans&nbsp;
can all coalesce around. Now, another thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:34.980
may damage McCarthy is that he seems unwilling&nbsp;
to impeach Joe Biden because he kind of gets the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:40.440
political ramifications that could happen if they&nbsp;
decide to impeach Biden for some absurd reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:44.280
especially because Biden hasn't really done&nbsp;
anything impeachable over these past couple of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:48.660
years, especially, you know, relatively speaking,&nbsp;
in terms of what you would impeach a president&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:53.460
for. Some say that he should be impeached for&nbsp;
pulling out of Afghanistan. Some on the right are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:58.740
saying that he should be impeached for inflation,&nbsp;
some say for Hunter Biden and the laptop from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:04.920
hell. It's clear the base just wants to impeach&nbsp;
Biden because of revenge. And they figure, okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:09.660
well, Trump got impeached twice, so we got to&nbsp;
impeach Biden in the same way. But that could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:14.280
end up backfiring hugely for them if it's seen as&nbsp;
being done for a nonsense reason. And I kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:19.800
get the sense that Kevin McCarthy understands that&nbsp;
he wants to avoid avoid impeachment altogether if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:24.480
he's able to. But if people are saying from&nbsp;
inside the House that you've got to do it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:27.960
if the constituents, the Republican Party,&nbsp;
you're saying you've got to do it, maybe his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:32.880
hand will be forced. You don't have to do that.&nbsp;
So Kevin McCarthy in a giant mess. I don't envy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:36.540
his position right now. It's not a shoo in that&nbsp;
he's going to become the next House speaker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:41.460
but he's certainly vying for it and we'll see if&nbsp;
it ends up happening. We're going to go to a break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:46.080
right now. We'll be back with much more of the&nbsp;
David Pakman show after these commercial messages.

